{"resourceIdentifier":"2750","resourceName":"Saving Colombia's Cloud Forest","description":"The tropical Andes are among the most vulnerable places on earth due to the dual impacts of climate change and human population pressures. With the intense and historic livestock activity in the area, deforestation in the valleys north and south have almost completely isolated the eastern forest. The remaining habitat is an oasis of biodiversity and one of Colombia’s many national treasures. The region also contains two important watersheds: the Río San Juan Antioquia, and Río San Juan Bravo Chocó.  These watersheds provide critical ecosystem services to communities living and working throughout the area and the forests are vital to protection of the headwaters of these important sources of fresh water to the surrounding communities. Without them, landslides, silting, and flooding threaten the health and safety of thousands of residents. \r\n\r\nAs our planet’s climate systems continue to be altered, it is highly likely that some or many of the species found in cloud forests will be lost forever. The effects of climate change are already apparent here. As a result, many cloud forest species have started to migrate upslope to maintain a favorable climate. However, their routes are often blocked by a degraded and fragmented landscape caused by agriculture moving up the mountainside and penetrating deeper into the lowlands.   \r\n\r\nOur goal is to create a large, protected area for endemic and threatened species to shield them from imminent threats, mitigate climate impacts, and prevent extinctions. Since 2017, we have worked closely with our local partner Fundación Colibri to create a vital connection across a narrow ridgeline. This ecological corridor will safeguard endemic and endangered wildlife and offers important protection for the headwaters of a major river, the primary source of fresh water for communities throughout the region.","location":{"latitude":5.488240,"longitude":-75.899890},"inPublicCommentPeriod":false,"lastPublicCommentPeriod":{"startInclusive":"2023-03-24","endExclusive":"2023-04-24","endInclusive":"2023-04-23"},"attributes":[{"code":"PROJECT_ID","values":[{"type":"string","value":"2750"}]},{"code":"STATE_PROVINCE","values":[{"type":"string","value":"Antioquia"}]}],"participationSummaries":[{"programCode":"VCS","attributes":[{"code":"PROPONENT_NAME","values":[{"type":"string","value":"Saving Nature, Inc."},{"type":"string","value":"North Carolina, United States"}]},{"code":"PROJECT_STATUS","values":[{"type":"string","value":"Registered"},{"type":"assetSearchLink","value":null,"targetId":"2750"},{"type":"bufferSearchLink","value":null,"targetId":"2750"}]},{"code":"EST_ANNUAL_EMISSION_REDCT","values":[{"type":"string","value":"7920"}]},{"code":"TOTAL_BUFFER_POOL_CREDITS","values":[{"type":"integer","value":354}]},{"code":"PRIMARY_PROJECT_CATEGORY_NAME","values":[{"type":"string","value":"Agriculture Forestry and Other Land Use"}]},{"code":"PROJECT_SUBCATERGORY_NAMES","values":[{"type":"string","value":"ARR"}]},{"code":"PROTOCOL_NAME","values":[{"type":"string","value":"AR-ACM0003"}]},{"code":"PROJECT_ACREAGE","values":[{"type":"string","value":"556 Hectares"}]},{"code":"VALIDATOR_NAME","values":[{"type":"string","value":"TÜV SÜD America Inc."}]},{"code":"PROJECT_REGISTRATION_DATE","values":[{"type":"string","value":"21/02/2025"}]},{"code":"CREDIT_PERIOD_INFO","values":[{"type":"string","value":"1st, 01/01/2017 - 31/12/2056"}]}]}],"documentGroups":[{"code":"VCS_PIPELINE_DOCUMENTS","description":"VCS Pipeline Documents","documents":[{"uri":"https://registry.verra.org/mymodule/ProjectDoc/Project_ViewFile.asp?FileID=78308&IDKEY=s98klasmf8jflkasf8098afnasfkj98f0a9sfsakjflsakjf8ds107986732","documentType":"Draft Project Description","documentName":"VCS-Project-Description-Template-v4.1_SavingNature_11_4_2022.pdf","uploadDate":"2022-11-04T23:10:40.08Z"},{"uri":"https://registry.verra.org/mymodule/ProjectDoc/Project_ViewFile